"math — Mathematical functions — Python 3.14.8 documentation (https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/math.html)\nciteturn59view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/math.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 550\nL0: [Input]\nL1: # `math` — Mathematical functionscite0†¶ L2: \nL3: * * *\nL4: \nL5: This module provides access to common mathematical functions and constants, including those defined by the C standard.\nL6: These functions cannot be used with complex numbers; use the functions of the same name from the cite1†`cmath` module if you require support for complex numbers. The distinction between functions which support complex numbers and those which don’t is made since most users do not want to learn quite as much mathematics as required to understand complex numbers.\nL7: Receiving an exception instead of a complex result allows earlier detection of the unexpected complex number used as a parameter, so that the programmer can determine how and why it was generated in the first place.\nL8: The following functions are provided by this module. Except when explicitly noted otherwise, all return values are floats.\nL9: Number-theoretic functions\nL10: cite2†`comb(n, k)` | Number of ways to choose k items from n items without repetition and without order\nL11: cite3†`factorial(n)` | n factorial\nL12: cite4†`gcd(*integers)` | Greatest common divisor of the integer arguments\nL13: cite5†`isqrt(n)` | Integer square root of a nonnegative integer n\nL14: cite6†`lcm(*integers)` | Least common multiple of the integer arguments\nL15: cite7†`perm(n, k)` | Number of ways to choose k items from n items without repetition and with order\nL16: Floating point arithmetic\nL17: cite8†`ceil(x)` | Ceiling of x, the smallest integer greater than or equal to x\nL18: cite9†`fabs(x)` | Absolute value of x\nL19: cite10†`floor(x)` | Floor of x, the largest integer less than or equal to x\nL20: cite11†`fma(x, y, z)` | Fused multiply-add operation: `(x * y) + z`\nL21: cite12†`fmod(x, y)` | Remainder of division `x / y`\nL22: cite13†`modf(x)` | Fractional and integer parts of x\nL23: cite14†`remainder(x, y)` | Remainder of x with respect to y\nL24: cite15†`trunc(x)` | Integer part of x\nL25: Floating point manipulation functions\nL26: cite16†`copysign(x, y)` | Magnitude (absolute value) of x with the sign of y\nL27: cite17†`frexp(x)` | Mantissa and exponent of x\nL28: cite18†`isclose(a, b, rel_tol, abs_tol)` | Check if the values a and b are close to each other\nL29: cite19†`isfinite(x)` | Check if x is neither an infinity nor a NaN\nL30: cite20†`isinf(x)` | Check if x is a positive or negative infinity\nL31: cite21†`isnan(x)` | Check if x is a NaN (not a number)\nL32: cite22†`ldexp(x, i)` | `x * (2**i)`, inverse of function cite17†`frexp()` L33: cite23†`nextafter(x, y, steps)` | Floating-point value steps steps after x towards y\nL34: cite24†`ulp(x)` | Value of the least significant bit of x\nL35: Power, exponential and logarithmic functions\nL36: cite25†`cbrt(x)` | Cube root of x\nL37: cite26†`exp(x)` | e raised to the power x\nL38: cite27†`exp2(x)` | 2 raised to the power x\nL39: cite28†`expm1(x)` | e raised to the power x, minus 1\nL40: cite29†`log(x, base)` | Logarithm of x to the given base (e by default)\nL41: cite30†`log1p(x)` | Natural logarithm of 1+x (base e)\nL42: cite31†`log2(x)` | Base-2 logarithm of x\nL43: cite32†`log10(x)` | Base-10 logarithm of x\nL44: cite33†`pow(x, y)` | x raised to the power y\nL45: cite34†`sqrt(x)` | Square root of x\nL46: Summation and product functions\nL47: cite35†`dist(p, q)` | Euclidean distance between two points p and q given as an iterable of coordinates\nL48: cite36†`fsum(iterable)` | Sum of values in the input iterable\nL49: cite37†`hypot(*coordinates)` | Euclidean norm of an iterable of coordinates\nL50: cite38†`prod(iterable, start)` | Product of elements in the input iterable with a start value\nL51: cite39†`sumprod(p, q)` | Sum of products from two iterables p and q\nL52: Angular conversion\nL53: cite40†`degrees(x)` | Convert angle x from radians to degrees\nL54: cite41†`radians(x)` | Convert angle x from degrees to radians\nL55: Trigonometric functions\nL56: cite42†`acos(x)` | Arc cosine of x\nL57: cite43†`asin(x)` | Arc sine of x\nL58: cite44†`atan(x)` | Arc tangent of x\nL59: cite45†`atan2(y, x)` | `atan(y / x)`\nL60: cite46†`cos(x)` | Cosine of x\nL61: cite47†`sin(x)` | Sine of x\nL62: cite48†`tan(x)` | Tangent of x\nL63: Hyperbolic functions\nL64: cite49†`acosh(x)` | Inverse hyperbolic cosine of x\nL65: cite50†`asinh(x)` | Inverse hyperbolic sine of x\nL66: cite51†`atanh(x)` | Inverse hyperbolic tangent of x\nL67: cite52†`cosh(x)` | Hyperbolic cosine of x\nL68: cite53†`sinh(x)` | Hyperbolic sine of x\nL69: cite54†`tanh(x)` | Hyperbolic tangent of x\nL70: Special functions\nL71: cite55†`erf(x)` | cite56†Error function†en.wikipedia.org at x\nL72: cite57†`erfc(x)` | cite56†Complementary error function†en.wikipedia.org at x\nL73: cite58†`gamma(x)` | cite59†Gamma function†en.wikipedia.org at x\nL74: cite60†`lgamma(x)` | Natural logarithm of the absolute value of the cite59†Gamma function†en.wikipedia.org at x\nL75: Constants\nL76: cite61†`pi` | π = 3.141592…\nL77: cite62†`e` | e = 2.718281…\nL78: cite63†`tau` | τ = 2π = 6.283185…\nL79: cite64†`inf` | Positive infinity\nL80: cite65†`nan` | “Not a number” (NaN)\nL81: ## Number-theoretic functionscite66†¶ L82: \nL83: math.comb(n, k)cite2†¶ L84: \nL85: \nL86: Return the number of ways to choose k items from n items without repetition and without order.\nL87: \nL88: Evaluates to `n! / (k! * (n - k)!)` when `k <= n` and evaluates to zero when `k > n`.\nL89: \nL90: Also called the binomial coefficient because it is equivalent to the coefficient of k-th term in polynomial expansion of `(1 + x)ⁿ`.\nL91: Raises cite67†`TypeError` if either of the arguments are not integers. Raises cite68†`ValueError` if either of the arguments are negative.\nL92: \nL93: Added in version 3.8.\nL94: \nL95: math.factorial(n)cite3†¶ L96: \nL97: \nL98: Return factorial of the nonnegative integer n.\nL99: \nL100: Changed in version 3.10: Floats with integral values (like `5.0`) are no longer accepted.\nL101: \nL102: math.gcd(*integers)cite4†¶ L103: Return the greatest common divisor of the specified integer arguments. If any of the arguments is nonzero, then the returned value is the largest positive integer that is a divisor of all arguments. If all arguments are zero, then the returned value is `0`. `gcd()` without arguments returns `0`.\nL104: \nL105: Added in version 3.5.\nL106: \nL107: Changed in version 3.9: Added support for an arbitrary number of arguments. Formerly, only two arguments were supported.\nL108: \nL109: math.isqrt(n)cite5†¶ L110: Return the integer square root of the nonnegative integer n. This is the floor of the exact square root of n, or equivalently the greatest integer a such that a² ≤ n.\nL111: \nL112: For some applications, it may be more convenient to have the least integer a such that n ≤ a², or in other words the ceiling of the exact square root of n. For positive n, this can be computed using `a = 1 + isqrt(n - 1)`.\nL113: \nL114: Added in version 3.8.\nL115: \nL116: math.lcm(*integers)cite6†¶ L117: Return the least common multiple of the specified integer arguments. If all arguments are nonzero, then the returned value is the smallest positive integer that is a multiple of all arguments. If any of the arguments is zero, then the returned value is `0`. `lcm()` without arguments returns `1`.\nL118: \nL119: Added in version 3.9.\nL120: \nL121: math.perm(n, k=None)cite7†¶ L122: \nL123: \nL124: Return the number of ways to choose k items from n items without repetition and with order.\nL125: Evaluates to `n! / (n - k)!` when `k <= n` and evaluates to zero when `k > n`.\nL126: \nL127: If k is not specified or is `None`, then k defaults to n and the function returns `n!`.\nL128: \nL129: Raises cite67†`TypeError` if either of the arguments are not integers. Raises cite68†`ValueError` if either of the arguments are negative.\nL130: \nL131: Added in version 3.8.\nL132: ## Floating point arithmeticcite69†¶ L133: \nL134: math.ceil(x)cite8†¶ L135: \nL136: \nL137: Return the ceiling of x, the smallest integer greater than or equal to x. If x is not a float, delegates to cite70†`x.__ceil__` , which should return an cite71†`Integral` value.\nL138: \nL139: math.fabs(x)cite9†¶ L140: \nL141: \nL142: Return the absolute value of x.\nL143: \nL144: math.floor(x)cite10†¶ L145: \nL146: \nL147: Return the floor of x, the largest integer less than or equal to x. If x is not a float, delegates to cite72†`x.__floor__` , which should return an cite71†`Integral` value.\nL148: math.fma(x, y, z)cite11†¶ L149: \nL150: \nL151: Fused multiply-add operation. Return `(x * y) + z`, computed as though with infinite precision and range followed by a single round to the `float` format. This operation often provides better accuracy than the direct expression `(x * y) + z`.\nL152: This function follows the specification of the fusedMultiplyAdd operation described in the IEEE 754 standard. The standard leaves one case implementation-defined, namely the result of `fma(0, inf, nan)` and `fma(inf, 0, nan)`. In these cases, `math.fma` returns a NaN, and does not raise any exception.\nL153: \nL154: Added in version 3.13.\nL155: \nL156: math.fmod(x, y)cite12†¶ L157: Return the floating-point remainder of `x / y`, as defined by the platform C library function `fmod(x, y)`. Note that the Python expression `x % y` may not return the same result. The intent of the C standard is that `fmod(x, y)` be exactly (mathematically; to infinite precision) equal to `x - n*y` for some integer n such that the result has the same sign as x and magnitude less than `abs(y)`.\nL158: Python’s `x % y` returns a result with the sign of y instead, and may not be exactly computable for float arguments. For example, `fmod(-1e-100, 1e100)` is `-1e-100`, but the result of Python’s `-1e-100 % 1e100` is `1e100-1e-100`, which cannot be represented exactly as a float, and rounds to the surprising `1e100`. For this reason, function `fmod()` is generally preferred when working with floats, while Python’s `x % y` is preferred when working with integers.\nL159: math.modf(x)cite13†¶ L160: \nL161: \nL162: Return the fractional and integer parts of x. Both results carry the sign of x and are floats.\nL163: \nL164: Note that `modf()` has a different call/return pattern than its C equivalents: it takes a single argument and return a pair of values, rather than returning its second return value through an ‘output parameter’ (there is no such thing in Python).\nL165: \nL166: math.remainder(x, y)cite14†¶ L167: Return the IEEE 754-style remainder of x with respect to y. For finite x and finite nonzero y, this is the difference `x - n*y`, where `n` is the closest integer to the exact value of the quotient `x / y`. If `x / y` is exactly halfway between two consecutive integers, the nearest even integer is used for `n`. The remainder `r = remainder(x, y)` thus always satisfies `abs(r) <= 0.5 * abs(y)`.\nL168: Special cases follow IEEE 754: in particular, `remainder(x, math.inf)` is x for any finite x, and `remainder(x, 0)` and `remainder(math.inf, x)` raise cite68†`ValueError` for any non-NaN x. If the result of the remainder operation is zero, that zero will have the same sign as x.\nL169: \nL170: On platforms using IEEE 754 binary floating point, the result of this operation is always exactly representable: no rounding error is introduced.\nL171: \nL172: Added in version 3.7.\nL173: \nL174: math.trunc(x)cite15†¶ L175: Return x with the fractional part removed, leaving the integer part. This rounds toward 0: `trunc()` is equivalent to cite10†`floor()` for positive x, and equivalent to cite8†`ceil()` for negative x. If x is not a float, delegates to cite73†`x.__trunc__` , which should return an cite71†`Integral` value.\nL176: For the cite8†`ceil()` , cite10†`floor()` , and cite13†`modf()` functions, note that all floating-point numbers of sufficiently large magnitude are exact integers. Python floats typically carry no more than 53 bits of precision (the same as the platform C double type), in which case any float x with `abs(x) >= 2**52` necessarily has no fractional bits.\nL177: ## Floating point manipulation functionscite74†¶ L178: \nL179: math.copysign(x, y)cite16†¶ L180: \nL181: \nL182: Return a float with the magnitude (absolute value) of x but the sign of y. On platforms that support signed zeros, `copysign(1.0, -0.0)` returns -1.0.\nL183: \nL184: math.frexp(x)cite17†¶ L185: Return the mantissa and exponent of x as the pair `(m, e)`. If x is a finite nonzero number, then m is a float with `0.5 <= abs(m) < 1.0` and an integer e is such that `x == m * 2**e` exactly. Else, return `(x, 0)`. This is used to “pick apart” the internal representation of a float in a portable way.\nL186: Note that `frexp()` has a different call/return pattern than its C equivalents: it takes a single argument and return a pair of values, rather than returning its second return value through an ‘output parameter’ (there is no such thing in Python).\nL187: \nL188: math.isclose(a, b, * (Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)), rel_tol=1e-09, abs_tol=0.0)cite18†¶ L189: \nL190: \nL191: Return `True` if the values a and b are close to each other and `False` otherwise.\nL192: Whether or not two values are considered close is determined according to given absolute and relative tolerances. If no errors occur, the result will be: `abs(a-b) <= max(rel_tol * max(abs(a), abs(b)), abs_tol)`.\nL193: rel_tol is the relative tolerance – it is the maximum allowed difference between a and b, relative to the larger absolute value of a or b. For example, to set a tolerance of 5%, pass `rel_tol=0.05`. The default tolerance is `1e-09`, which assures that the two values are the same within about 9 decimal digits. rel_tol must be nonnegative and less than `1.0`.\nL194: abs_tol is the absolute tolerance; it defaults to `0.0` and it must be nonnegative. When comparing `x` to `0.0`, `isclose(x, 0)` is computed as `abs(x) <= rel_tol  * abs(x)`, which is `False` for any nonzero `x` and rel_tol less than `1.0`. So add an appropriate positive abs_tol argument to the call.\nL195: The IEEE 754 special values of `NaN`, `inf`, and `-inf` will be handled according to IEEE rules. Specifically, `NaN` is not considered close to any other value, including `NaN`. `inf` and `-inf` are only considered close to themselves.\nL196: \nL197: Added in version 3.5.\nL198: \nL199: See also\nL200: \nL201: cite75†PEP 485†peps.python.org – A function for testing approximate equality\nL202: \nL203: math.isfinite(x)cite19†¶ L204: \nL205: \nL206: Return `True` if x is neither an infinity nor a NaN, and `False` otherwise. (Note that `0.0` is considered finite.)\nL207: Added in version 3.2.\nL208: \nL209: math.isinf(x)cite20†¶ L210: \nL211: \nL212: Return `True` if x is a positive or negative infinity, and `False` otherwise.\nL213: \nL214: math.isnan(x)cite21†¶ L215: \nL216: \nL217: Return `True` if x is a NaN (not a number), and `False` otherwise.\nL218: \nL219: math.ldexp(x, i)cite22†¶ L220: \nL221: \nL222: Return `x * (2**i)`. This is essentially the inverse of function cite17†`frexp()` .\nL223: \nL224: math.nextafter(x, y, steps=1)cite23†¶ L225: \nL226: \nL227: Return the floating-point value steps steps after x towards y.\nL228: \nL229: If x is equal to y, return y, unless steps is zero.\nL230: \nL231: Examples:\nL232:   * `math.nextafter(x, math.inf)` goes up: towards positive infinity.\nL233: \nL234:   * `math.nextafter(x, -math.inf)` goes down: towards minus infinity.\nL235: \nL236:   * `math.nextafter(x, 0.0)` goes towards zero.\nL237: \nL238:   * `math.nextafter(x, math.copysign(math.inf, x))` goes away from zero.\nL239: \nL240: See also cite24†`math.ulp()` .\nL241: \nL242: Added in version 3.9.\nL243: \nL244: Changed in version 3.12: Added the steps argument.\nL245: \nL246: math.ulp(x)cite24†¶ L247: \nL248: \nL249: Return the value of the least significant bit of the float x:\nL250: \nL251:   * If x is a NaN (not a number), return x.\nL252:   * If x is negative, return `ulp(-x)`.\nL253: \nL254:   * If x is a positive infinity, return x.\nL255: \nL256:   * If x is equal to zero, return the smallest positive denormalized representable float (smaller than the minimum positive normalized float, cite76†`sys.float_info.min` ).\nL257: \nL258:   * If x is equal to the largest positive representable float, return the value of the least significant bit of x, such that the first float smaller than x is `x - ulp(x)`.\nL259:   * Otherwise (x is a positive finite number), return the value of the least significant bit of x, such that the first float bigger than x is `x + ulp(x)`.\nL260: \nL261: ULP stands for “Unit in the Last Place”.\nL262: \nL263: See also cite23†`math.nextafter()` and cite76†`sys.float_info.epsilon` .\nL264: \nL265: Added in version 3.9.\nL266: ## Power, exponential and logarithmic functionscite77†¶ L267: \nL268: math.cbrt(x)cite25†¶ L269: \nL270: \nL271: Return the cube root of x.\nL272: \nL273: Added in version 3.11.\nL274: \nL275: math.exp(x)cite26†¶ L276: \nL277: \nL278: Return e raised to the power x, where e = 2.718281… is the base of natural logarithms. This is usually more accurate than `math.e ** x` or `pow(math.e, x)`.\nL279: \nL280: math.exp2(x)cite27†¶ L281: \nL282: \nL283: Return 2 raised to the power x.\nL284: \nL285: Added in version 3.11.\nL286: \nL287: math.expm1(x)cite28†¶ L288: Return e raised to the power x, minus 1. Here e is the base of natural logarithms. For small floats x, the subtraction in `exp(x) - 1` can result in a cite78†significant loss of precision†en.wikipedia.org ; the `expm1()` function provides a way to compute this quantity to full precision:\nL289: \nL290:     >>> from math import exp, expm1\nL291:     >>> exp(1e-5) - 1  # gives result accurate to 11 places\nL292:     1.0000050000069649e-05\nL293:     >>> expm1(1e-5)    # result accurate to full precision\nL294:     1.0000050000166668e-05\nL295: Added in version 3.2.\nL296: \nL297: math.log(x[, base])cite29†¶ L298: \nL299: \nL300: With one argument, return the natural logarithm of x (to base e).\nL301: \nL302: With two arguments, return the logarithm of x to the given base, calculated as `log(x)/log(base)`.\nL303: \nL304: math.log1p(x)cite30†¶ L305: \nL306: \nL307: Return the natural logarithm of 1+x (base e). The result is calculated in a way which is accurate for x near zero.\nL308: \nL309: math.log2(x)cite31†¶ L310: \nL311: \nL312: Return the base-2 logarithm of x. This is usually more accurate than `log(x, 2)`.\nL313: \nL314: Added in version 3.3.\nL315: \nL316: See also\nL317: cite79†`int.bit_length()` returns the number of bits necessary to represent an integer in binary, excluding the sign and leading zeros.\nL318: \nL319: math.log10(x)cite32†¶ L320: \nL321: \nL322: Return the base-10 logarithm of x. This is usually more accurate than `log(x, 10)`.\nL323: \nL324: math.pow(x, y)cite33†¶ L325: Return x raised to the power y. Exceptional cases follow the IEEE 754 standard as far as possible. In particular, `pow(1.0, x)` and `pow(x, 0.0)` always return `1.0`, even when x is a zero or a NaN. If both x and y are finite, x is negative, and y is not an integer then `pow(x, y)` is undefined, and raises cite68†`ValueError` .\nL326: \nL327: Unlike the built-in `**` operator, `math.pow()` converts both its arguments to type cite80†`float` . Use `**` or the built-in `pow()` function for computing exact integer powers.\nL328: Changed in version 3.11: The special cases `pow(0.0, -inf)` and `pow(-0.0, -inf)` were changed to return `inf` instead of raising cite68†`ValueError` , for consistency with IEEE 754.\nL329: \nL330: math.sqrt(x)cite34†¶ L331: \nL332: \nL333: Return the square root of x.\nL334: ## Summation and product functionscite81†¶ L335: \nL336: math.dist(p, q)cite35†¶ L337: \nL338: \nL339: Return the Euclidean distance between two points p and q, each given as a sequence (or iterable) of coordinates. The two points must have the same dimension.\nL340: \nL341: Roughly equivalent to:\nL342: \nL343:     sqrt(sum((px - qx) ** 2.0 for px, qx in zip(p, q)))\nL344: \nL345: \nL346: Added in version 3.8.\nL347: \nL348: math.fsum(iterable)cite36†¶ L349: Return an accurate floating-point sum of values in the iterable. Avoids loss of precision by tracking multiple intermediate partial sums.\nL350: \nL351: The algorithm’s accuracy depends on IEEE-754 arithmetic guarantees and the typical case where the rounding mode is half-even. On some non-Windows builds, the underlying C library uses extended precision addition and may occasionally double-round an intermediate sum causing it to be off in its least significant bit.\nL352: For further discussion and two alternative approaches, see the cite82†ASPN cookbook recipes for accurate floating-point summation†code.activestate.com .\nL353: \nL354: math.hypot(*coordinates)cite37†¶ L355: \nL356: \nL357: Return the Euclidean norm, `sqrt(sum(x**2 for x in coordinates))`. This is the length of the vector from the origin to the point given by the coordinates.\nL358: \nL359: For a two dimensional point `(x, y)`, this is equivalent to computing the hypotenuse of a right triangle using the Pythagorean theorem, `sqrt(x*x + y*y)`.\nL360: Changed in version 3.8: Added support for n-dimensional points. Formerly, only the two dimensional case was supported.\nL361: \nL362: Changed in version 3.10: Improved the algorithm’s accuracy so that the maximum error is under 1 ulp (unit in the last place). More typically, the result is almost always correctly rounded to within 1/2 ulp.\nL363: \nL364: math.prod(iterable, * (Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)), start=1)cite38†¶ L365: Calculate the product of all the elements in the input iterable. The default start value for the product is `1`.\nL366: \nL367: When the iterable is empty, return the start value. This function is intended specifically for use with numeric values and may reject non-numeric types.\nL368: \nL369: Added in version 3.8.\nL370: \nL371: math.sumprod(p, q)cite39†¶ L372: \nL373: \nL374: Return the sum of products of values from two iterables p and q.\nL375: \nL376: Raises cite68†`ValueError` if the inputs do not have the same length.\nL377: Roughly equivalent to:\nL378: \nL379:     sum(map(operator.mul, p, q, strict=True))\nL380: \nL381: \nL382: For float and mixed int/float inputs, the intermediate products and sums are computed with extended precision.\nL383: \nL384: Added in version 3.12.\nL385: ## Angular conversioncite83†¶ L386: \nL387: math.degrees(x)cite40†¶ L388: \nL389: \nL390: Convert angle x from radians to degrees.\nL391: \nL392: math.radians(x)cite41†¶ L393: \nL394: \nL395: Convert angle x from degrees to radians.\nL396: ## Trigonometric functionscite84†¶ L397: \nL398: math.acos(x)cite42†¶ L399: \nL400: \nL401: Return the arc cosine of x, in radians. The result is between `0` and `pi`.\nL402: \nL403: math.asin(x)cite43†¶ L404: \nL405: \nL406: Return the arc sine of x, in radians. The result is between `-pi/2` and `pi/2`.\nL407: \nL408: math.atan(x)cite44†¶ L409: \nL410: \nL411: Return the arc tangent of x, in radians. The result is between `-pi/2` and `pi/2`.\nL412: \nL413: math.atan2(y, x)cite45†¶ L414: Return `atan(y / x)`, in radians. The result is between `-pi` and `pi`. The vector in the plane from the origin to point `(x, y)` makes this angle with the positive X axis. The point of `atan2()` is that the signs of both inputs are known to it, so it can compute the correct quadrant for the angle. For example, `atan(1)` and `atan2(1, 1)` are both `pi/4`, but `atan2(-1, -1)` is `-3*pi/4`.\nL415: \nL416: math.cos(x)cite46†¶ L417: \nL418: \nL419: Return the cosine of x radians.\nL420: \nL421: math.sin(x)cite47†¶ L422: \nL423: \nL424: Return the sine of x radians.\nL425: math.tan(x)cite48†¶ L426: \nL427: \nL428: Return the tangent of x radians.\nL429: ## Hyperbolic functionscite85†¶ L430: \nL431: cite86†Hyperbolic functions†en.wikipedia.org are analogs of trigonometric functions that are based on hyperbolas instead of circles.\nL432: \nL433: math.acosh(x)cite49†¶ L434: \nL435: \nL436: Return the inverse hyperbolic cosine of x.\nL437: \nL438: math.asinh(x)cite50†¶ L439: \nL440: \nL441: Return the inverse hyperbolic sine of x.\nL442: \nL443: math.atanh(x)cite51†¶ L444: \nL445: \nL446: Return the inverse hyperbolic tangent of x.\nL447: \nL448: math.cosh(x)cite52†¶ L449: \nL450: \nL451: Return the hyperbolic cosine of x.\nL452: \nL453: math.sinh(x)cite53†¶ L454: \nL455: \nL456: Return the hyperbolic sine of x.\nL457: math.tanh(x)cite54†¶ L458: \nL459: \nL460: Return the hyperbolic tangent of x.\nL461: ## Special functionscite87†¶ L462: \nL463: math.erf(x)cite55†¶ L464: \nL465: \nL466: Return the cite56†error function†en.wikipedia.org at x.\nL467: \nL468: The `erf()` function can be used to compute traditional statistical functions such as the cite88†cumulative standard normal distribution†en.wikipedia.org :\nL469: \nL470:     def phi(x):\nL471:         'Cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution'\nL472:         return (1.0 + erf(x / sqrt(2.0))) / 2.0\nL473: \nL474: \nL475: Added in version 3.2.\nL476: \nL477: math.erfc(x)cite57†¶ L478: Return the complementary error function at x. The cite56†complementary error function†en.wikipedia.org is defined as `1.0 - erf(x)`. It is used for large values of x where a subtraction from one would cause a cite78†loss of significance†en.wikipedia.org .\nL479: \nL480: Added in version 3.2.\nL481: \nL482: math.gamma(x)cite58†¶ L483: \nL484: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nsubprocess — Subprocess management — Python 3.14.8 documentation (https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/subprocess.html)\nciteturn59view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://docs.python.org/3.14/library/subprocess.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 920\n"
